  "shortDescription": {
    "en": "A young woman named Mara Vale leaves a narrowed city life she has learned to make look smaller than it is, from an apartment whose one narrow window faces a brick wall. An invitation from Celia Rowan asks her to come north. Bellwether is a small town of one main street, a bakery, a hardware store, and hills under a very large sky. An imperfect house at the edge of town has a leaking roof and an attic room. The invitation poses the question of whether a horizon can be remembered."
  },
  "aboutBook": {
    "en": [
      "Beyond the Last Star begins with Mara Vale, already skilled at making her life look smaller and at saying she is fine. A narrow window, a silence, and a larger sky follow.",
      "Mara Vale leaves the city skilled at making her life look smaller than it is. Her mother's questions receive the same two words, and those words ask nothing of anyone. She is fine. That smoothness is the stake.",
      "A year earlier she prepared museum presentations and met Eli under the old train station clock. Now the museum has her resignation, the silence between them stands as an answer, and the photographs she catalogued remain in a storage box she cannot open.",
      "The bus sets Mara beneath a hand-painted sign in Bellwether: one main street, a bakery, a hardware store, hills rising under a sky so large it seems theatrical. Arrival, not a verdict, is the approach.",
      "Five flights above a laundromat, one narrow window faces a brick wall that turns violet at dusk and then black, while washing machines tumble below. The fit is quiet attention, a coat still on.",
      "Celia waits in a battered green truck, her yellow coat bright enough that Mara recognizes her at once. At the orchard's edge the Rowan house leaks into buckets, and the attic opens onto a field of stars.",
      "A smaller life, a silence taken as an answer, a bus under that theatrical sky, a yellow coat, and stars above a leaking roof: what is still being asked?"
    ]
  },
